\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Image volume and pushforward mass}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{On the Borel real line, let #{T(x)=2x}. The
[Jacobian theorem](fdip-002K) gives #{J(T)=2}, but its two measure
directions have reciprocal coefficients:
##{\lambda_{\mathbb R}(T[0,1])=2,\qquad
 T_*\lambda_{\mathbb R}([0,1])=\tfrac12.}
The first set is #{[0,2]}, whereas the second expression measures the
preimage #{[0,1/2]}. This directly checks the convention of
\citet{Exercise 1.2.21, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Mathlib's \leanref{Real.map_volume_mul_left} requires a nonzero scalar
and gives the reciprocal absolute pushforward factor.}
\p{Put #{q_V(x)=x^2} and #{q_W(y)=y^2/4}. Then
#{q_W(Tx)=q_V(x)} although #{J(T)=2}. Preserving a quadratic form
alone does not preserve the supplied reference volume.}
\p{If a Borel weight #{w\ge0} has a finite positive integral
#{Z=\int w\,d\lambda_V}, replacing the reference by #{c\lambda_V},
#{c>0}, replaces #{Z} by #{cZ}. Consequently
#{(cZ)^{-1}w\,d(c\lambda_V)=Z^{-1}w\,d\lambda_V}.
This conditional cancellation follows from linearity for indicators,
simple functions and monotone limits. It proves neither existence nor
finiteness of #{Z}; the reference and the action are separate data.}
